<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp1sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>Multiplication by 0 and <m>-1</m></title>
        <p>
            In this investigation, you continue to deduce important algebraic properties for fields once taken for granted in <m>\R</m>.
        </p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-field-multiypling_zero">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field and <m>a\in F</m>, then <m>a0=0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum <m>a0+a0</m>. You may find field properties (F5), (F2), and (F3) in <xref ref="def-field"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-field-additive_inverses">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field and <m>a\in F</m>, then <m>(-1)a=-a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum <m>(1)a+(-1)a</m>. You may find field properties (F2) through (F5) in <xref ref="def-field"/>, as well as <xref ref="prop-field-multiypling_zero"/> and <xref ref="prop-field-addititive_inverse_uniqueness"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-fields_integral_domains">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>F</m> is a field and <m>a,b\in F</m> satisfy <m>ab=0</m>, then either <m>a=0</m> or <m>b=0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof by cases. Case 1: <m>a=0</m>. Case 2: <m>a\ne 0</m>. You may find certain field properties in  <xref ref="def-field"/>, as well as <xref ref="prop-field-multiypling_zero"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>


</section>
